{
  "schema": "feed.case/0.1",
  "caseId": "ut-triga-pulse-4p40",
  "name": "TRIGA $4.40 pulse, hot channel, built from UT-Austin SAR numbers (illustrative)",
  "label": "synthetic",
  "citation": "Properties and geometry from the University of Texas TRIGA II SAR, Chapter 4 (8/2023): fuel k 0.18 W/cm/K and heat capacity 2.04 + 0.00417 T[C] J/cm3/K (Table 4.1; the K-State SAR Table 13.3 gives the same law as 340.1 + 0.6952 T[C] J/kg/K at 5996 kg/m3), diameters and the 38.1 cm fuel length (Table 4.1), the 6.35 mm zirconium fill rod (Figure 4.30), element axial peaking 1.017 (Table 4.17). Cladding heat capacity 7900 kg/m3 x 477 J/kg/K at 300 K from the K-State SAR Table 13.3. Pulse numbers: peak hot-channel power about 8.9e4 kW at $4.40 (Figure 4.32, READ FROM THE FIGURE) and maximum fuel temperature 824 C (section 4.6). Gap conductance 4096 W/m2/K and the SAR's TRACE wall temperature are not used; the gap value is read from Figure 4.30 (see ut-triga-hot-channel-annular).",
  "description": "ILLUSTRATIVE, not a reproduction of the SAR. The SAR gives the peak power and the peak temperature of a $4.40 pulse but not the power-versus-time curve, so the curve here is ASSUMED to be sech^2 (the exact shape of an adiabatic Nordheim-Fuchs excursion) with the width chosen so that the energy deposited takes the fuel from 20 C to 824 C if no heat leaves: 3.069 MJ/m, 237.6 MW/m at the peak (8.9e4 kW over 0.381 m x 1.017), FWHM 11.4 ms. A pulse width of about 10 ms is what a $4 TRIGA pulse looks like, so the SAR's three numbers are consistent; that is a plausibility check, not a validation. The peak temperature is 824 C by construction (the interior is adiabatic over a pulse this short). What the case shows is the effect of the temperature-dependent heat capacity (the fuel's heat capacity roughly doubles over the pulse) and the cool-down after it. The film coefficient is an assumed 10 kW/m2/K. Thermal only: the power history is an input, FEED does not compute the pulse.",
